{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The argument fails because it equates 'severity' with a probabilistic expectation ('would likely fail'), whereas Popper defines severity logically based on the improbability of the prediction relative to background knowledge, not an inductive generalization about nature. A test is severe if the theory forbids what background knowledge permits; this is a deductive relation between statements, not an inductive inference about future regularities. By claiming severity 'presupposes regularities,' the target attacks a straw man of Popper's logic rather than his actual demarcation criterion, which relies on the content of the theory, not the probability of its survival.","premise":"The target conflates the logical definition of test severity with the psychological expectation of failure, misrepresenting Popper's criterion as inherently inductive.","successor_question":"If severity is purely a logical relation between a theory and background knowledge, does the preference for corroborated theories require any assumption about the uniformity of nature?","target":"2ce96850cf3b98254f35978fc170840648cb0478603829b00edb9692d05ab1a5"}]}